8. ELECTRICAL MAINTENANCE MANAGEMENT
==================================================

This is an important module.

Build it around the actual electrical maintenance workflow in a manufacturing factory.

FAULT REPORTING:

Allow operators and supervisors to report electrical faults.

Information required:

- Machine name.
- Machine ID.
- Fault category.
- Fault description.
- Date.
- Time.
- Reported by.
- Production shift.
- Machine alarm information, if available.
- Severity.
- Current status.

Example fault categories:

- Photoelectric sensor failure.
- Proximity sensor failure.
- Heater failure.
- Temperature controller failure.
- Electrical plug failure.
- Socket failure.
- Power supply issue.
- Wiring problem.
- Other electrical fault.

These categories must be configurable.

MAINTENANCE RECORDS:

Create a digital version of the electrical maintenance logbook.

Record:

- Date.
- Machine name.
- Fault description.
- Action taken.
- Spare parts used.
- Downtime duration.
- Running hours.
- Servicing hours.
- Technician responsible.
- Maintenance start time.
- Maintenance completion time.
- Machine restoration time.
- Additional observations.

Allow technicians to update maintenance records.

MAINTENANCE HISTORY:

Each machine must have a searchable electrical maintenance history.

Users should be able to identify:

- Previous electrical faults.
- Previous troubleshooting actions.
- Spare parts previously used.
- Repeated faults.
- Maintenance frequency.
- Downtime history.

SHIFT-END REPORTING:

At the end of each shift, electrical maintenance personnel must be able to generate a maintenance report.

The report should summarize:

- Total faults reported.
- Faults resolved.
- Unresolved faults.
- Maintenance activities.
- Spare parts used.
- Total downtime.
- Running hours.
- Servicing hours.
- Important observations.

Allow the technician to submit the report to the Maintenance Head.

The Maintenance Head should have a dashboard showing submitted reports and reports awaiting submission.

